1">sum [height] of trees-on forest1 / ( (Max-Treeheight ) * count forest1) * 100</monitor><monitor x="1094" y="81" width="59" height="45" precision="2" fontSize="11" display="Forest 2">sum [height] of trees-on forest2 / ( (Max-Treeheight) * count forest2) * 100</monitor><monitor x="1095" y="130" width="57" height="45" precision="2" fontSize="11" display="Total">sum [height] of trees / (Max-Treeheight * count patches) * 100</monitor><chooser x="10" y="410" height="60" variable="Learning-rule" current="0" width="190" display="Learning-rule"><choice type="string" value="toward payoff"></choice><choice type="string" value="no learning"></choice><choice type="string" value="away from payoff"></choice></chooser><slider x="210" y="482" width="172" height="33" min="0" max="2" default="1.0" step="0.1" direction="Horizontal" display="Learning-rate" variable="Learning-rate"/><slider x="390" y="482" width="172" height="33" min="5" max="100" default="25.0" step="5" direction="Horizontal" display="Block-length" variable="Block-length" units="ticks"/><slider x="570" y="482" width="172" height="33" min="0" max="30" default="10.0" step="1" direction="Horizontal" display="Exploration" variable="Exploration" units="points"/><monitor x="750" y="482" width="80" height="45" precision="1" fontSize="11" display="Level F1 (%)">[cut-level] of one-of farmers1</monitor><monitor x="836" y="482" width="80" height="45" precision="1" fontSize="11" display="Level F2 (%)">[cut-level] of one-of farmers2</monitor><monitor x="922" y="482" width="95" height="45" precision="2" fontSize="11" display="Predicted F1">[last-S] of one-of farmers1</monitor><monitor x="1022" y="482" width="95" height="45" precision="2" fontSize="11" display="Observed F1">[last-change] of one-of farmers1</monitor><monitor x="1122" y="482" width="95" height="45" precision="2" fontSize="11" display="Predicted F2">[last-S] of one-of farmers2</monitor><monitor x="1222" y="482" width="95" height="45" precision="2" fontSize="11" display="Observed F2">[last-change] of one-of farmers2</monitor><plot x="210" y="535" width="389" height="222" xMin="0.0" xMax="10.0" yMin="0.0" yMax="100.0" autoPlotX="true" autoPlotY="false" legend="true" display="Harvest level (percent)"><setup></setup><update></update><pen display="Forester 1" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-10899396" legend="true"><setup></setup><update>plot [cut-level] of one-of farmers1</update></pen><pen display="Forester 2" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-2674135" legend="true"><setup></setup><update>plot [cut-level] of one-of farmers2</update></pen></plot><plot x="676" y="535" width="413" height="222" xMin="-20.0" xMax="20.0" yMin="-20.0" yMax="20.0" autoPlotX="true" autoPlotY="true" legend="true" display="Predicted vs observed change"><setup></setup><update></update><pen display="Forester 1" interval="1.0" mode="2" color="-10899396" legend="true"><setup></setup><update></update></pen><pen display="Forester 2" interval="1.0" mode="2" color="-2674135" legend="true"><setup></setup><update></update></pen></plot></widgets>
<info># Two Foresters with learning

This is a research variant of the Two Foresters model. The classroom model is unchanged. Here each forester learns: the harvest percentage is no longer fixed by a slider. Drafted with AI assistance, open for review.

## What a forester does
Each tick a forester tries a harvest percentage close to its current typical level, plus or minus some exploration. The wood it takes that tick is its payoff. After each block of ticks (Block-length) it looks at how the percentages it tried and the wood it got moved together, and shifts its typical level.

## The calculation shown
After each block the model computes S, the covariance of harvest percent and wood taken, divided by the mean wood taken. This is the selection term of the covariance based law of effect in Borgstede and Eggert (2021), with the payoff itself as the predictor of benefit and a slope of 1. The law says the mean percentage of the next block should differ from this block's mean by S. The plot "Predicted vs observed change" shows S on the horizontal axis and the observed change on the vertical axis. Points on the diagonal mean the prediction was right.

## Learning-rule
* toward payoff: the typical level moves by Learning-rate times S.
* no learning: the typical level stays put (a control).
* away from payoff: the level moves by minus Learning-rate times S (a control).

## Things to try
* Private forest on, then off, with both foresters starting at the same level. What happens to the two levels and to the forest?
* Learning-rate 0 against 1.
* Does the wood taken now still predict wealth later? Run the BehaviorSpace experiment proxy-validity.

## Limits
The model is a toy. The payoff is wood taken in one tick, which is an immediate stand-in for long-term wealth. Results are simulated and say nothing about real foresters or real forests.

## Source
Original model: Two Foresters, OpenEvo / TeachingBase (CC BY-SA 4.0). Borgstede, M., and Eggert, F. (2021). The formal foundation of an evolutionary theory of reinforcement. Behavioural Processes, 186, 104370. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.beproc.2021.104370
</info>
